    "evidence": "> Barrett’s esophagus may be present in up to 5% of high-risk patients with gastroesophageal\n> reflux disease (e.g., older white men) undergoing upper endoscopy. The risk of progression\n> to dysplasia or cancer may be related to the length of Barrett’s epithelium. Therefore, it is\n> important to characterize and document the length and location of the salmon-colored\n> mucosa during EGD.",
