    "pages": "P.274 – P.275",
    "category": "Imaging & Procedures",
    "description": "Fetoscopy is an endoscopic procedure which allows direct visualization of the fetus.\nThe procedure can be done for diagnostic and/or therapeutic reasons. Fetoscopy is\ndone to evaluate the fetus for birth defects and to collect fetal blood and tissue\n(skin) samples. Fetoscopy can also be done for therapeutic purposes such as laser\nocclusion of abnormal vessels. Because the procedure involves inserting the feto-\nscope through the abdominal wall into the amniotic cavity, there are serious risks,\nincluding miscarriage, bleeding, and preterm labor. Because of these risks,\nfetoscopy is done only if there is a high probability that the fetus is not normal or\nthere is a strong family history of birth defects. Typically other testing such as fetal\nultrasound, amniocentesis, or chorionic villus sampling are the preferred testing\nmethods whenever possible.",
