    "title": "Fibrin Degradation Products (FDPs, Fibrin Split Products [FSPs], Fibrin Breakdown Products [FBPs])",
    "aliases": "FDPs, Fibrin Split Products [FSPs], Fibrin Breakdown Products [FBPs]",
    "pages": "P.275 – P.277",
    "category": "Hematology & Coagulation",
    "description": "During hemostasis, fibrin, with the addition of fibrin stabilizing factor, forms a stable\nfibrin clot at the site of injury. Once the fibrin clot is no longer needed, it is dissolved\nby fibrinolytic agents such as plasmin, resulting in fibrin degradation products (FDPs).\nTheir presence in the blood is indicative of recent clotting activity. FDPs have an anti-\ncoagulant effect and inhibit clotting.\n\nMeasurement of FDPs provides information regarding the activity of the clot-\ndissolving system. When high levels of FDPs are present, they indicate increased\nfibrinolysis, as seen in disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC). This test is\nused in the diagnosis of DIC and acute occlusive vascular disease.",
