    "category": "Urinalysis & Renal Clearance",
    "description": "Leucine aminopeptidase (LAP) is an enzyme normally found in the hepatocytes of\nthe liver and is present in the blood, bile, and urine. It is released into the blood\nafter damage to liver cells from hepatotoxic drugs or infection, such as hepatitis.\nLAP may also be released into the blood by tumors in the liver, thus it may also serve\nas a tumor marker.\n\nThis test is useful in diagnosis of conditions when the alkaline phosphatase is\nelevated. LAP changes parallel those of alkaline phosphatase except that LAP is\nusually normal in bone diseases or malabsorption problems, but serves as an indi-\ncator of liver damage. LAP is generally not as sensitive as testing other liver\nenzymes such as ALT, AST, ALP, LDH, and GGT. Unlike other liver enzymes LAP can\nalso be measured in the urine.",
