    "id": 262,
    "title": "Porphyrins (Coproporphyrin, Porphobilinogen [PBG], Uroporphyrin, Urinary Porphyrins)",
    "aliases": "Coproporphyrin, Porphobilinogen [PBG], Uroporphyrin, Urinary Porphyrins",
    "pages": "P.463 – P.464",
    "category": "Urinalysis & Renal Clearance",
    "description": "Several substances known as “porphyrins,” including porphobilinogen (PBG), uro-\nporphyrin, and coproporphyrin, are involved in the synthesis of heme of hemoglo-\nbin. Each of these steps requires the presence of a specific enzyme. If any of the\nenzymes are deficient (because of a genetic disease or interference by a toxic sub-\nstance), the intermediate substances build up, and a type of porphyria results. With\na disturbance of the heme synthesis pathway, as in the case of porphyria, large\namounts of porphyrins are excreted. Since porphyrins are considered urine pig-\nments, their presence causes the urine to be amber to burgundy in color.",
    "evidence": "",
