    "aliases": "",
    "pages": "P.475 – P.476",
    "category": "Urinalysis & Renal Clearance",
    "description": "Pregnanetriol is involved in the synthesis of adrenal corticoids. It is a metabolite of\n17-hydroxyprogesterone and is normally excreted in the urine in very small\namounts. In adrenogenital syndrome, cortisol synthesis is blocked at the point at\nwhich 17-hydroxyprogesterone converts to cortisol. This results in a build up of 17-\nhydroxyprogesterone and increased amounts of its metabolite, pregnanetriol, are\nexcreted in the urine.\n\nThe reduced plasma cortisol levels stimulate the secretion of ACTH, which nor-\nmally leads to increased cortisol levels. However, since cortisol synthesis is impaired,\npregnanetriol levels continue to rise instead. High levels of 17-hydroxyprogesterone\nand ACTH lead to virilization in females and sexual precocity in young males.",
