    "contributingFactors": "• Prolactin levels are temporarily increased following exercise, stress, or a recent\nbreast examination.\n• Diagnostic tests using radioactive materials, recent surgery, or hemolysis of the\nblood sample may alter test results.\n• Drugs which may increase prolactin levels: antipsychotics, cimetidine, clomipramine,\ncocaine, danazol, enalapril, furosemide, insulin, labetelol, megestrol, methyldopa,\nmetoclopramide, morphine, oral contraceptives, phenytoin, risperidone, tricyclic anti-\ndepressants, verapamil.\n• Drugs which may decrease prolactin levels: anticonvulsants, bromocriptine, calci-\ntonin, cyclosporine, dexamethasone, estrogens, finasteride, levodopa, metoclo-\npramide, morphine, nifedipine, octreotide, phenytoin, tamoxifen.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"test-274-prostate-sonogram\"></a>",
