    "contributingFactors": "• Falsely elevated PSA values occur following palpation of the prostate or any manip-\nulation such as through cystoscopy, transrectal ultrasound, or prostatic biopsy.\n• PSA values may be affected after recent urinary tract infection or bladder\ncatheterization.\n• Drug which may increase PSA level: allopurinol.\n• Drug which may decrease PSA level: finasteride.",
    "clinicalAlerts": "> [!WARNING]\n> • If the total PSA is elevated but the DRE is normal, the free PSA test may be\n> ordered.\n> • This test can help to distinguish between prostate cancer and non-cancer\n> causes of elevated PSA.\n> • PSA associated with cancer is more protein bound; free, or non-protein\n> bound PSA increases in the case of benign prostatic hypertrophy.\n> • Free PSA >27% has a lower likelihood of prostate cancer.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"test-276-protein-c\"></a>",
