    "id": 296,
    "title": "Rubella Antibody Test",
    "aliases": "German Measles Test",
    "pages": "P.521 – P.522",
    "category": "Immunology & Serology",
    "description": "Rubella, also known as the German measles or 3-day measles, is a virus that is not\nusually considered a serious condition. It usually causes fever and transient rash in\nchildren and adults who contract it. Rubella is serious, however, if a woman con-\ntracts the disease during the first trimester of pregnancy. It can cause miscarriage,\nstillbirth, and congenital defects such as deafness, microcephaly, and heart\ndefects. Thus, it is extremely important for women considering pregnancy to be\ntesting for susceptibility or immunity to rubella.\n\nThe rubella antibody test measures IgG and IgM antibody formation against the\nrubella virus. It can be used to diagnose rubella and to determine whether the per-\nson is immune to the virus. A diagnosis of rubella is made when a fourfold increase\nbetween the acute titer and the convalescent titer occurs. Demonstration of specific\nIgG on a serum sample is evidence of immunity to rubella.",
    "evidence": "",
