    "id": 331,
    "title": "Total Iron-Binding Capacity",
    "aliases": "TIBC",
    "pages": "P.573 – P.575",
    "category": "Hematology & Coagulation",
    "description": "Iron in the body is found in several locations. A large portion (approximately 65%)\nis carried in the red blood cells by hemoglobin. Another 4% is found in myoglobin\nin the skeletal muscle. Ferritin stores in the liver, bone marrow, and spleen account\nfor almost 30% of the total iron. The remaining iron is in transit throughout the\nbody, bound to transferrin and other iron binding serum proteins.\n\nTotal iron-binding capacity (TIBC) measures the total amount of iron that trans-\nferrin can bind. It is usually measured along with serum iron when investigating a\npossible iron deficiency anemia. When iron stores are low, the TIBC is usually\nhigher than normal. The test can also be performed when iron overload is sus-\npected. A calculation of serum iron divided by TIBC provides a percentage known\nas transferrin saturation, a useful indicator of iron status.",
