    "description": "Bilirubin, which is one of the components of bile, is formed in the liver, spleen, and\nbone marrow. It is also formed as a result of hemoglobin breakdown. There are three\ntypes of bilirubin: total, direct (conjugated), and indirect (unconjugated).\n\nDirect, or conjugated, bilirubin is converted to urobilinogen by intestinal bac-\nteria in the duodenum. The majority of urobilinogen is excreted in the stool. The\nliver reprocesses the remaining urobilinogen into bile. A very small amount is\nexcreted in the urine.\n\nThe fecal urobilinogen level is dependent upon the amount of conjugated biliru-\nbin excreted in the bile salts into the intestine. Fecal urobilinogen levels decrease\nwhen obstructive jaundice (as from gallstones) or hepatic jaundice occurs, since the\nbilirubin is unable to reach the intestines for excretion and instead enters the blood-\nstream for excretion by the kidneys.\n\nThere must also be adequate numbers of bacteria present in the intestine to\nbreakdown the bilirubin into urobilinogen. With inadequate bacterial presence, as\n\noccurs following oral antibiotic therapy, the decreased urobilinogen in the feces\nresults in a light-colored stool.",
