    "clinicalAlerts": "> [!WARNING]\n> • When suspecting AIP, urine porphyrin studies should also be done.\n> • If the patient is found to have acute intermittent porphyria, provide teaching regard-\n> ing factors which have been found to precipitate acute episodes, including: drugs\n> and hormones (barbiturates, chlordiazepoxide, ergot, estrogens, glutethimide,\n> griseofulvin, meprobamate, methyprylon, phenytoin, steroid hormones, and sulfon-\n> amides), low-carbohydrate diets, alcohol consumption, and infections.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"test-351-vanillylmandelic-acid-and-catecholamines-vma-dopamine-epinephrine-norepinephrine-metanephrine-normetanephrine\"></a>",
