    "category": "Body Fluids & CSF",
    "description": "A viral culture involves collecting a small sample of body tissue or fluid and plac-\ning it in a cell culture. The virus is allowed to replicate to help with identification\nof the specific virus. It is best for samples to be collected during the acute phase\nof a condition.\n\nSources of specimens for culturing include blood, cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), res-\npiratory secretions, stool, tissue, urine, eyes, and lesions of the oral mucosa, rectal\nmucosa, skin, and genitals. The types of viruses able to be detected include aden-\novirus, cytomegalovirus, herpes simplex virus, influenza, mumps, respiratory syncy-\ntial virus (RSV), varicella-zoster virus, and enteroviruses such as coxsackie virus and\npoliovirus. The length of time from specimen collection until diagnosis varies from\nhours to days, depending on the virus and the culture method used.",
